<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Belz Voivodeship</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1462">1462</a>–<a href="/years/1772">1772</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belz">Belz</a> Voivodeship was a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a>, and later of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, created in 1462 when King Casimir IV took over the Principality of Belz after the local line of the Masovian dukes died out. With its capital at Belz, it lay in the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-ruthenia">Red Ruthenia</a>, in present-day western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> annexed it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/belz-voivodeship.jpg" alt="Belz Voivodeship" width="320" height="280" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Poznaniak · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RON_wojew%C3%B3dztwo_be%C5%82skie_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
